// Sorting lines — and the distinction between a **key** and an **order**, which nothing can say.
//
// Third file from the 264 shipped sources nobody predicted anything about, picked because sorting is
// the one thing in `packages/box` that is not I/O. `packages/box/src/lib/lines.wac` is 149 lines and
// two of them are a design nobody has a word for.

import { Result, Bytes, Vec, Ordering, SortSpec, sorted, uniqueByKey, bytesCmp } from "core";

// **`cmpBytes` is gone.** It was *"byte order — negative, zero or positive, as every comparator in
// every language"*, and it is `../../../core/bytesCmp` now, answering `Ordering`. Fifth witness for
// that function after `@/packages/tor/src/vote.wac`, `@/packages/tor/src/hsdir.wac` and
// `@/packages/box/src/lib/lines.wac` in the shipped tree, which write it three times identically.

/**
 * The integer a line starts with, after blanks, or zero — **saturating**, and the reason is a fixed
 * bug that the fix reintroduces further out.
 *
 * The shipped doc:
 *
 * > **i64, and saturating.** In i32 it wrapped: `sort -n` over `4294967296 1 2147483648 -1` answered
 * > `2147483648 -1 4294967296 1`, and `-nu` merged `4294967296` with `0` because both keys came out
 * > the same — a distinct line silently dropped. Saturation rather than wrapping keeps the order
 * > monotonic for input wider than i64 as well; GNU compares those exactly, which needs arbitrary
 * > precision and is a bigger change than this bug.
 *
 * Read that twice. The bug was **two distinct lines getting the same key**, and the fix is a rule
 * that gives two distinct lines the same key — every integer past `i64` max is one key now. What
 * changed is the *threshold* and the *ordering*: wrapping made the order non-monotonic, which is
 * wrong for `sort`; saturation keeps it monotonic, which is right for `sort` and still wrong for
 * `-u`.
 *
 * So the fix is correct and the class of bug survives it, and the file knows — *"GNU compares those
 * exactly, which needs arbitrary precision"*. `@/packages/bignum` is in this tree and is not reachable
 * from an applet's sort key, because a key that allocates is a key you cannot compare in a loop.
 */
i64 leadingNumber(Bytes b) { … }

/**
 * The numeric key.
 *
 * **Not the sort order**, and the difference is the finding. The shipped doc:
 *
 * > `cmpNumericThenBytes` is the comparison a sort wants; this one is the *key*, and `-u` is where
 * > the difference shows.
 * >
 * > Separate from `cmpNumeric` because `-u` must not use it: uniqueness is the key's, so `1` and
 * > `01` are one line, and the survivor is the first in input order rather than the first in byte
 * > order.
 */
export Ordering cmpNumeric(Bytes a, Bytes b) { … }

/**
 * `sort -n`, as one value — **and `cmpNumericThenBytes` no longer exists.**
 *
 * The shipped file has a second function, *"the key, then the whole line — GNU's last-resort
 * comparison, which makes `sort -n` deterministic"*, and a paragraph explaining how it relates to the
 * first. `SortSpec.order()` **is** that function, composed from the two fields, so the pair that
 * needed a paragraph is now one declaration and the derived one is derived. §3.
 */
export SortSpec<Bytes> numeric() { return SortSpec(cmpNumeric, bytesCmp); }

/** `sort` with no `-n`: the whole line, and nothing to break ties with. */
export SortSpec<Bytes> byBytes() { return SortSpec.total(bytesCmp); }

// ## 1. A key and an order are different things and no library here has both
//
// `sort -n` needs two comparisons over the same lines:
//
//   * **the key**, `cmpNumeric`, which decides *are these the same line for `-u`* — `1` and `01` are;
//   * **the order**, `cmpNumericThenBytes`, which decides *which comes first* and must be total, so
//     equal keys fall back to the whole line.
//
// The second **refines** the first: `cmpNumeric(a, b) != 0` implies `cmpNumericThenBytes(a, b)` has
// the same sign. That relation is the whole contract between them, it is what makes `sort -nu`
// correct, and it is stated nowhere except in the two function names and a paragraph.
//
// Every language's sorting library has this hole. `sortBy` takes one comparator; `distinctBy` takes
// a key; nothing takes the pair and nothing checks that the order refines the key. Where it bites is
// exactly `-u`: hand `sortWith` the *order* and then drop adjacent equals, and `1` and `01` survive
// as two lines, because they are unequal under the order and equal under the key. The shipped
// applet gets this right by calling two different functions in two places, and a reader has to know
// which is which.
//
// **What a type would look like** — and it is a library type, not a language feature:
//
//     struct SortSpec<T> { fn<i32(T, T)> key; fn<i32(T, T)> tieBreak; }
//
// with `order()` composing them and `sameKey()` exposed for `-u`, so a caller cannot reach the order
// when it wanted the key. What the *language* would have to add is nothing; what it cannot do is
// check the refinement, which is a property of two functions and is not expressible.
//
// **Written, 2026-09-05, as `../../../core/order.wac`, and adopted here** — see §3 for what the
// second consumer of that file found. The sketch above got one thing wrong and it is the thing this
// directory says most often: `fn<i32(T, T)>` is a closed set spelled as an integer.
//
// The answer being a **library** gap rather than a language one is what makes it worth the entry —
// this directory mostly reaches *the type could say it if the language allowed*, and here the type
// can say it and nobody wrote the type.
//
// (A first draft called this the *first* such finding in 125 entries. It is not, and the nearest
// counterexample is two entries away in `../../../QUESTIONS.md`, written the same day. Fourth bare
// ordinal over an unnamed set I have written today.)
//
// ## 3. What adopting `core/order.wac` did, and one of the two functions stopped existing
//
// **`cmpNumericThenBytes` is gone.** It was a whole exported function whose entire content was *the
// key, and then the line*, sitting beside a paragraph explaining its relation to `cmpNumeric`. That
// relation is `SortSpec.order()`, so the pair is one declaration —
// `SortSpec(cmpNumeric, bytesCmp)` — and the second function is **derived rather than written**.
//
// That is more than the entry above claimed. It argued the type would stop a caller reaching the
// order when it meant the key; what it actually does is remove the opportunity, because there is no
// second name to reach for. The `-u` path is `uniqueByKey(sorted(xs, spec), spec)` and both halves
// take the same value.
//
// **`cmpBytes` is gone too**, into `core` — and it is the *fifth* witness for that function, three of
// them in the shipped tree writing the same eight lines (`tor/vote.wac`, `tor/hsdir.wac`,
// `box/lib/lines.wac`). Filed as `issues/system/0345a`.
//
// **And the cost the new type predicted lands exactly here.** `../../../core/order.wac` argues that
// `Ordering` is a payload-free enum, that an enum is one wasm struct, and that
// `spec/spec/enums.md` records payload-free variants being **freshly constructed** at every mention
// — so a comparator answering `Ordering` allocates per comparison. This file is the one place in the
// directory that runs a comparator *n log n* times. The file that proposed the type named the cost
// in the abstract; this is the consumer where it is a number, and neither file can measure it.
//
// > **Second consumer, second time the type moved.** `@/packages/rlp` made `core/cursor.wac` grow a
// > `need(n)` and disproved its claim about `sub`. This one deletes an exported function and
// > inherits an allocation. Neither was predicted by the sweep that produced either type.
//
// **What did not move:** stability. `sorted` promises it and this file relied on it before, so the
// adoption changes nothing here — and that is only true because the promise was written into the
// signature *because* of this file. Reversed, it would be the silent behaviour change
// `issues/system/0345a` is filed about.
//
// ## 2. What could not be written
//
// **A comparator is a funcref because there are no traits**, which `../../../QUESTIONS.md` has from
// `Map.create()`'s side. `sortWith` is the second site and it is the one where the shipped code
// deleted the convenience wrapper and said why: *"a wrapper saving one argument, for one caller that
// does not want it, is a name that documents nothing"*. So the funcref is not felt as a cost here —
// one caller, two comparators, and the parameter is the point. Recorded as the counterweight to the
// `Map` entry, which reads as though funcrefs were uniformly a burden.
//
// **~~`cmpBytes(a, b) == 0` is how equality is spelled~~ — answered 2026-09-05, and the answer has a
// shape.** `Slice.eq` and `core`'s `bytesEq` exist now. Four witnesses in the end, not two: this
// file, `@/packages/box/src/applets/diff.wac`'s inner loop (twice per iteration),
// `@/packages/tor/src/onionaddr.wac`, which wrote two `get`s instead, and `core/map.wac`, which
// declared its own `bytesEq` while arguing about hashing and did not notice it was a fifth site for
// something else.
//
// The length check alone is most of the win — two slices of different lengths are unequal in one
// comparison, and `cmpBytes` **cannot** know that, because a three-way answer has to say which is
// smaller and that needs the bytes.
//
// And it took a comparator, which is the finding rather than a wart. `Slice<T>` cannot ask `T`
// whether two of them are equal: no traits, no constraints on type parameters. So
// `eq(other, same)` is not a convenience API, it is the only spelling — and this file's *other*
// entry says the funcref is fine for a comparator and `core/map.wac` says it is a burden for a key.
// **Both are right and the difference is whether the caller has one to hand**: `sortWith` is called
// with the comparator the caller already chose, and `Map.create` is called with two functions that
// are the same two functions every time. The funcref is a cost exactly when it is a constant.
//
// **Stability is load-bearing and is a property of the algorithm, not the signature.** *"Stable,
// which `sort -u` then relies on to drop only adjacent equals"* — so replacing `sortWith` with a
// faster unstable sort silently changes which duplicate survives, and nothing in the type says so.
// The same shape as `@/packages/zstd`'s `Buf` invariant: a caller's correctness resting on a callee's
// choice, with the sentence in the callee.
